\pard\qc\cf2\b\fs16 IB PHYSICS IA REPORT TEMPLATE | STUDENT WORKING DOCUMENT\b0\par
\pard\sa160\cf1\b\fs32 IB Physics Internal Assessment\line Report Template\b0\par
\pard\sa120\cf2\i\fs22 A structure for planning and writing your scientific investigation\i0\par
\pard\sa160\highlight4\b HOW TO USE THIS TEMPLATE\b0  Replace the grey guidance with your own writing and delete all guidance before final submission. The template supports your structure; it does not write the investigation for you.\highlight0\par

\pard\sa80\cf1\b\fs20 IA AT A GLANCE\b0\par
\pard\sa40\fs20\b 20%\b0  of final IB Physics grade   |   \b 24 marks\b0  (4 criteria x 6)   |   \b 3000 words maximum\b0   |   \b SL & HL: same IA requirements\b0\par
\pard\sa140\fs20 Final report: PDF. 11-12 pt Arial recommended. No title page or table of contents required. Do not put your name inside the final report. Include student code, word count and page numbers. Reference accepted values, non-standard equations, diagrams and external sources.\par

\page
\pard\cf1\b\fs28 1. Descriptive title\b0\par
\pard\cf2\b\fs18 Suggested emphasis: brief\b0\par
\pard\i\fs20 Write a specific title that tells the reader what relationship or physical system is being investigated. Avoid titles such as "Physics IA" or "Pendulum experiment".\i0\par
\pard\sa160\fs20 [TYPE YOUR DESCRIPTIVE TITLE HERE]\par

\pard\cf1\b\fs28 2. Research question\b0\par
\pard\cf2\b\fs18 Suggested emphasis: brief\b0\par
\pard\i\fs20 State one focused question with one clear independent variable, one measurable dependent variable and a specific physical system. Make the relationship quantifiable.\i0\par
\pard\sa160\fs20 [TYPE YOUR RESEARCH QUESTION HERE]\par

\pard\cf1\b\fs28 3. Background physics and theoretical model\b0\par
\pard\cf2\b\fs18 Suggested emphasis: moderate\b0\par
\pard\i\fs20 Explain only the physics needed to understand and predict the relationship. Define important quantities and symbols, state the relevant model/equation, explain the predicted relationship and identify important assumptions. Avoid general history and unnecessary derivations of standard equations.\i0\par
\pard\cf1\b\fs20 Key equation / model\b0\par
\pard\sa120\fs20 [INSERT AND EXPLAIN THE RELEVANT EQUATION(S)]\par
\pard\cf1\b\fs20 Predicted relationship and assumptions\b0\par
\pard\sa160\fs20 [EXPLAIN WHAT THE MODEL PREDICTS AND THE ASSUMPTIONS IT RELIES ON]\par

\pard\cf1\b\fs28 4. Variables and methodology\b0\par
\pard\cf2\b\fs18 Suggested emphasis: substantial\b0\par
\pard\i\fs20 Explain the decisions behind the investigation. The reader should understand what you changed, what you measured, what mattered enough to control, and why the chosen range/equipment/method is suitable.\i0\par

\pard\cf1\b\fs20 Variables\b0\par
\pard\fs20 Independent variable: [WHAT YOU CHANGE, RANGE, UNITS AND WHY]\par
\pard\fs20 Dependent variable: [WHAT YOU MEASURE/CALCULATE, UNITS AND HOW]\par
\pard\fs20 Controlled variables: [IMPORTANT PHYSICAL QUANTITIES, HOW AND WHY THEY ARE CONTROLLED]\par

\pard\cf1\b\fs20 Apparatus and measurement decisions\b0\par
\pard\i\fs20 List the important apparatus, but also explain measurement resolution/uncertainty and why the chosen instrument or method is suitable. Include a labelled setup diagram or clear photograph where useful.\i0\par
\pard\sa140\fs20 [APPARATUS + MEASUREMENT DECISIONS]\par

\pard\cf1\b\fs20 Range, values and repeats\b0\par
\pard\i\fs20 State the planned range and spacing of the independent variable and justify it. For a straightforward linear relationship, school guidance uses at least five IV values as a minimum; use more where appropriate. State how many repeats are made and why.\i0\par
\pard\sa140\fs20 [RANGE + NUMBER OF VALUES + REPEATS]\par

\pard\cf1\b\fs20 Method\b0\par
\pard\i\fs20 Write a reproducible method describing what you actually did. Include setup, how the IV is set, how the DV is measured, repeats, controls, calibration/alignment/zeroing and how you move to the next IV value. Be detailed but concise.\i0\par
\pard\fs20 1. [STEP]\par
\pard\fs20 2. [STEP]\par
\pard\fs20 3. [STEP]\par
\pard\fs20 4. [STEP]\par
\pard\fs20 5. [STEP]\par
\pard\fs20 6. [STEP]\par

\pard\cf1\b\fs20 Safety and practical considerations\b0\par
\pard\i\fs20 Address genuine risks briefly and specifically. If there is no meaningful risk beyond normal laboratory practice, state that briefly rather than inventing hazards.\i0\par
\pard\sa160\fs20 [SAFETY / RISK INFORMATION]\par

\page
\pard\cf1\b\fs28 5. Raw data\b0\par
\pard\cf2\b\fs18 Suggested emphasis: moderate\b0\par
\pard\i\fs20 Present raw measurements clearly. Put units and uncertainties in table headings, keep precision consistent, and show enough data to support the conclusion. If a data logger produces many values, summarise appropriately rather than pasting pages of output.\i0\par
\pard\sa160\fs20 [INSERT RAW DATA TABLE(S) HERE]\par
\pard\cf1\b\fs20 Qualitative observations (only if useful)\b0\par
\pard\sa160\fs20 [OBSERVATIONS]\par

\pard\cf1\b\fs28 6. Data processing and uncertainty\b0\par
\pard\cf2\b\fs18 Suggested emphasis: substantial\b0\par
\pard\i\fs20 Show how raw data become the quantities used in your analysis. Include means, transformations and uncertainty processing where appropriate. Show one worked example for a non-trivial calculation; do not repeat the same calculation for every row.\i0\par
\pard\cf1\b\fs20 Example calculation\b0\par
\pard\sa140\fs20 [SHOW ONE REPRESENTATIVE CALCULATION]\par
\pard\cf1\b\fs20 Processed data\b0\par
\pard\sa160\fs20 [INSERT PROCESSED DATA TABLE(S) HERE]\par

\pard\cf1\b\fs28 7. Graphs and analysis\b0\par
\pard\cf2\b\fs18 Suggested emphasis: substantial\b0\par
\pard\i\fs20 Insert graphs large enough to read. Label axes with units, use error bars where appropriate, and choose the graph from the physics model rather than forcing a straight line. Interpret the trend, gradient/intercept or fitted parameter and explain what the uncertainty means for the model.\i0\par
\pard\cf1\b\fs20 Graph 1: [DESCRIPTIVE TITLE]\b0\par
\pard\qc\sa180\fs22 [INSERT GRAPH HERE]\par
\pard\cf1\b\fs20 Analysis of the graph\b0\par
\pard\sa120\fs20 [INTERPRET THE TREND, GRADIENT, INTERCEPT, ERROR BARS AND UNCERTAINTY]\par
\pard\i\fs19 Useful checks: Does theory predict the intercept? Is the relationship linear or only approximately linear? Is linearisation justified? What is the uncertainty in the gradient where appropriate? Do not use only the first and last points to estimate the gradient.\i0\par

\page
\pard\cf1\b\fs28 8. Conclusion\b0\par
\pard\cf2\b\fs18 Suggested emphasis: substantial\b0\par
\pard\i\fs20 Answer the research question directly. Use quantitative evidence from your processed data/graph, compare with accepted theory or a referenced accepted value/range where appropriate, and explain whether agreement is meaningful in light of uncertainty. State the range over which your conclusion applies.\i0\par
\pard\cf1\b\fs20 Direct answer to the research question\b0\par
\pard\sa120\fs20 [YOUR QUANTITATIVE ANSWER]\par
\pard\cf1\b\fs20 Comparison with theory / accepted value\b0\par
\pard\sa120\fs20 [COMPARE WITH ACCEPTED PHYSICS AND REFERENCE THE SOURCE]\par
\pard\cf1\b\fs20 Role of uncertainty and scope\b0\par
\pard\sa160\fs20 [EXPLAIN WHAT THE UNCERTAINTY ALLOWS YOU TO CONCLUDE AND THE RANGE OF VALIDITY]\par

\pard\cf1\b\fs28 9. Evaluation\b0\par
\pard\cf2\b\fs18 Suggested emphasis: substantial\b0\par
\pard\i\fs20 Prioritise a few important limitations rather than listing many generic problems. Build each point from evidence in your own data: Evidence -> Limitation -> Impact -> Improvement. Distinguish random uncertainty from systematic effects and model limitations. Avoid "human error" and do not fill the section with strengths.\i0\par
\pard\cf1\b\fs20 Evaluation point 1\b0\par
\pard\fs20 Evidence: [WHAT IN YOUR DATA SHOWS A PROBLEM?]\par
\pard\fs20 Limitation: [SPECIFIC CAUSE]\par
\pard\fs20 Impact: [HOW IT AFFECTS GRADIENT / INTERCEPT / UNCERTAINTY / VALIDITY]\par
\pard\sa120\fs20 Improvement: [REALISTIC CHANGE THAT TARGETS THE PROBLEM]\par
\pard\cf1\b\fs20 Evaluation point 2\b0\par
\pard\fs20 Evidence: [ ]\par
\pard\fs20 Limitation: [ ]\par
\pard\fs20 Impact: [ ]\par
\pard\sa120\fs20 Improvement: [ ]\par
\pard\cf1\b\fs20 Evaluation point 3\b0\par
\pard\fs20 Evidence: [ ]\par
\pard\fs20 Limitation: [ ]\par
\pard\fs20 Impact: [ ]\par
\pard\sa160\fs20 Improvement: [ ]\par
\pard\cf1\b\fs20 Overall scope / next step\b0\par
\pard\sa160\fs20 [STATE A PRECISE BOUNDARY OR GENUINE NEXT INVESTIGATION]\par

\pard\cf1\b\fs28 10. References\b0\par
\pard\cf2\b\fs18 Suggested emphasis: brief\b0\par
\pard\i\fs20 List the sources actually used. Reference accepted values, non-standard equations, diagrams/images and other external material. Use a consistent referencing style required by your school/teacher.\i0\par
\pard\sa160\fs20 [REFERENCES]\par

\page
\pard\cf1\b\fs28 Final submission checklist\b0\par
\pard\i\fs20 Use this after you have deleted all template guidance and before exporting the final PDF.\i0\par
\pard\fs20 - Descriptive title, student code, word count and page numbers are present.\par
\pard\fs20 - No student name appears inside the final report.\par
\pard\fs20 - Report is no more than 3000 words.\par
\pard\fs20 - RQ has one clear IV and one measurable DV.\par
\pard\fs20 - Relevant physics is explained without unnecessary background.\par
\pard\fs20 - Methodology choices, range, controls and measurement uncertainty are justified.\par
\pard\fs20 - Raw data tables include units and uncertainties; significant figures are realistic.\par
\pard\fs20 - Graphs are readable and based on the physics model.\par
\pard\fs20 - Conclusion answers the RQ using quantitative evidence and accepted physics.\par
\pard\fs20 - Evaluation uses Evidence -> Limitation -> Impact -> Improvement.\par
\pard\fs20 - Any outlier removal is justified.\par
\pard\fs20 - References are complete.\par
\pard\fs20 - No "human error" or generic "take more measurements" without explanation.\par
\pard\fs20 - Final PDF opens correctly and all figures, tables, graphs and equations are readable.\par
\pard\sa100\cf1\b\fs20 FINAL FILENAME\b0\par
\pard\b\fs22 First name Second name Physics IA wordcount.pdf\b0\par
\pard\cf2\fs19 Example: Alex Morgan Physics IA 2980.pdf\par
